WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.740
Incident response for wormable prompt injection starts with the propagation
path. Which untrusted content entered the system? Which model or agent
processed it? What output was generated?

2
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:16.740
Where was that output stored, sent, indexed, or copied? Which downstream
workflows consumed it?

3
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.460
This timeline matters more than arguing whether the model was fooled in an
abstract sense.

4
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:30.740
Containment should reduce spread quickly. Disable risky connectors if they
can write, send, or synchronize. Pause automated actions that depend on
model-generated instructions.

5
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:42.420
Quarantine suspicious documents, messages, tickets, and retrieved chunks. If
memory or RAG indexes may be poisoned, stop using them for high-risk tasks
until they are reviewed or rebuilt.

6
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.460
Credentials and permissions need a separate pass.

7
00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:53.940
If an agent had access to mail, storage, tickets, source control, cloud
resources, or business APIs, review what it could do and what it actually
did.

8
00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:01.300
Rotate exposed credentials where appropriate. Revoke unnecessary tokens.
Tighten scopes that allowed broad reads or writes.

9
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.780
The incident is not only about text; it is about authority granted to
automation.

10
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:15.860
Logs should answer operational questions. What sources were retrieved? Which
tools were called? What destinations received output? Which user approved or
did not approve the action?

11
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:23.140
What memory entries were read or written? Without this telemetry, defenders
may be forced to guess which surfaces are contaminated.

12
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:31.140
The best logs connect model activity to application decisions, so
investigators can distinguish a strange answer from a workflow action that
changed state.

13
00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:44.660
Recovery should include design changes, not only cleanup. Add provenance,
approval gates, connector restrictions, retrieval hygiene, output
validation, and monitoring for unusual copying or forwarding behavior.

14
00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:50.100
A good post-incident review names the broken trust boundary and the missing
control that allowed propagation.

15
00:01:50.180 --> 00:02:00.100
Tabletop exercises are useful here because teams can rehearse connector
shutdown, index quarantine, token rotation, and communication before a real
incident forces those choices.
